// ============================================================================
// FRAMEWORK-BEHAVIOR · DISPATCH  (the hop no call site expresses)
//
// ── WHY THIS IS ITS OWN PHASE ───────────────────────────────────────────────
// The counterpart of java/engine/framework-behavior/, and it inherits that folder's
// contract verbatim:
//
//   - a rule here models a LIBRARY'S BEHAVIOUR, not the language, so no interpreter
//     can adjudicate it. CPython cannot be asked whether `send_report.delay(k)` runs
//     `send_report`; only a running worker can. Every framework NAME a rule keys on
//     therefore lives in config-resolution/knobs.dl and nowhere else, and NO RULE IN
//     THIS FILE MENTIONS A FRAMEWORK BY NAME;
//   - a rule here may only ADD relations. It must never write call_chain_edge or any
//     relation the resolution phases own. A framework model is a weaker kind of
//     knowledge than a resolved call, and folding the two together would silently
//     restate every existing consumer in terms of it;
//   - it is included AFTER call-edge-generation because it CONSUMES the resolved graph.
//
// ── WHAT IS MISSING WITHOUT IT ──────────────────────────────────────────────
// Python had exactly one framework rule: config-resolution/entry-points.dl marks an
// HTTP route handler as an entry point. Six mechanisms in wide use connect two client
// declarations with no call site between them, and every rule in call-edge-generation
// is silent across each one:
//
//   the producer writes              the consumer is                    joined by
//   send_report.delay(k)             @shared_task def send_report       a proxy object
//   def test_total(cart)             @fixture def cart                  a PARAMETER NAME
//   path("items/<k>/", detail)       def detail(request, k)             a table entry
//   order_placed.send(sender=o)      @receiver(order_placed) def on_..  a SIGNAL OBJECT
//   svc = Depends(get_service)       def get_service()                  a default value
//   @listens_for(User, "before_..")  the decorated function             a registration
//
// So `impact send_report` answers "nothing the graph can see" for a body that 493 call
// sites dispatch on one public project, and test-impact cannot reach a test through the
// fixture it injects. That is the same shape #778 reported for routes.
//
// ── WHAT IT EMITS, AND WHY NOT AS A CALL ────────────────────────────────────
//   framework_edge(FromMethod, ToMethod, Mechanism, Detail, Confidence)
//     Mechanism   task_dispatch | fixture_injection | url_dispatch |
//                 signal_dispatch | di_provider | orm_hook
//     Detail      what joined the two ends: the dispatch method, the parameter name,
//                 the route table, the signal binding, the marker, the hook target
//     Confidence  registered   both ends carry the framework's own registration
//                 by_name      the join is a NAME the framework resolves at run time
//                              (a fixture parameter), so it is correct exactly when
//                              the runner's scoping agrees with lexical scope
//
// A framework hop is NOT an invocation: it may cross a process (a task body runs in a
// worker), it may be asynchronous, and it carries different confidence. Emitting it as
// call_chain_edge would change the meaning of every existing consumer, so it is a
// separate relation that a consumer opts into, exactly as Java's remote_edge is.
//
// A declaration the framework calls from OUTSIDE the client also gets an entry_point
// row, which is a different claim: "this runs, with no caller here" rather than "these
// two are connected".
// ============================================================================

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 0. MATCHING A DECORATOR BY ITS TAIL
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Every catalogue in knobs.dl is a TAIL, because the same registration is written with
// and without a receiver: `@shared_task` and `@app.task(...)` and `@queue.task` are one
// mechanism. Souffle has no rfind, so the tail is TESTED rather than extracted, the way
// config-resolution/entry-points.dl tests a route verb.
//
// The receiver half is deliberately unconstrained: the author names it (app, queue,
// celery, event, v1) and pinning that list would miss the next spelling. What keeps the
// match honest is never the name alone -- it is the shape each rule requires around it.
.decl py_fw_deco_tail(v:symbol)
py_fw_deco_tail(v) :- py_task_register_deco(v).
py_fw_deco_tail(v) :- py_fixture_deco(v).
py_fw_deco_tail(v) :- py_signal_receiver_deco(v).
py_fw_deco_tail(v) :- py_orm_hook_deco(v).

// py_deco_named(DecoratorHash, Tail) -- the decorator's dotted path IS the tail, or
// ends with "." ++ tail. A bare `@task` and `@app.task` both match; `@mytask` does not,
// because a tail must be a whole dotted segment.
.decl py_deco_named(h:symbol, v:symbol)
py_deco_named(h, v) :- decorator_text("client", dp, _, _, h), py_fw_deco_tail(v), dp = v.
py_deco_named(h, v) :- decorator_text("client", dp, _, _, h), py_fw_deco_tail(v),
    strlen(dp) > strlen(v) + 1,
    substr(dp, strlen(dp) - strlen(v), strlen(v)) = v,
    substr(dp, strlen(dp) - strlen(v) - 1, 1) = ".".

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 1. TASK DISPATCH  (producer -> worker)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// A registration decorator REPLACES the decorated name with a proxy. That is why the
// body is unreachable and also why it is findable: the producer's receiver is a binding
// whose `def` was replaced, and binding_binds_replaced_method holds exactly that def.
.decl py_task_registered(m:symbol)
py_task_registered(m) :- decorator_target("client", _, m, h), m != "",
    py_deco_named(h, v), py_task_register_deco(v).

// The worker invokes the body; nothing in the client does.
entry_point(m, "task") :- py_task_registered(m).

// `<name>.delay(...)` / `<name>.apply_async(...)` where <name> is that replaced def.
//
// THREE CONDITIONS, each load-bearing:
//   a catalogued dispatch method   `delay` alone is an ordinary method name
//   a NAME receiver                 the proxy is reached through a binding, not an
//                                   arbitrary expression
//   the binding's def is REGISTERED this is the whole test; an undecorated def, or a
//                                   parameter, produces nothing
.decl py_task_dispatch(site:symbol, from:symbol, to:symbol, meth:symbol)
py_task_dispatch(site, from, to, meth) :-
    call_decl("client", _, meth, _, _, site), py_task_dispatch_method(meth),
    call_receiver("client", "NAME", _, rexpr, site),
    call_context("client", _, from, _, _, site), from != "",
    expr_binding("client", b, ctx, rexpr), ctx != "STORE",
    binding_lookup("client", b, b2),
    binding_binds_replaced_method("client", b2, to),
    py_task_registered(to).

framework_edge(from, to, "task_dispatch", meth, "registered") :-
    py_task_dispatch(_, from, to, meth).

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 2. FIXTURE INJECTION  (test -> fixture)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The runner matches a test's PARAMETER NAME against the declared fixtures and calls
// the fixture to build the argument. The name is the entire join, so the confidence is
// `by_name`: it is correct exactly where the runner's scoping agrees with lexical scope.
.decl py_fixture_decl(m:symbol, name:symbol)
py_fixture_decl(m, name) :- decorator_target("client", _, m, h), m != "",
    py_deco_named(h, v), py_fixture_deco(v),
    method_decl("client", name, _, _, _, m).

// The runner calls a requested fixture; nothing in the client does.
entry_point(m, "fixture") :- py_fixture_decl(m, _), py_fixture_requested(m).

// A collected test: the runner recognises it by the name it was given.
.decl py_collected_test(m:symbol)
py_collected_test(m) :- method_decl("client", name, _, _, _, m), py_test_name_prefix(p),
    strlen(name) >= strlen(p), substr(name, 0, strlen(p)) = p.

// A REQUESTER is a test, or a fixture -- a fixture may request another, and the chain
// has to carry or `impact` stops one hop short.
.decl py_fixture_requester(m:symbol)
py_fixture_requester(m) :- py_collected_test(m).
py_fixture_requester(m) :- py_fixture_decl(m, _).

// THE NEGATIVE CONDITIONS, and each one is what stops a name coincidence:
//   the owner must be a requester   an ordinary helper taking `cart` is not a test
//   the parameter must have NO default  a defaulted parameter is supplied by the
//                                   caller; the runner injects nothing
//   the parameter must not be a receiver  `self` is not a fixture request
//   some fixture must declare the name  an unmatched parameter claims nothing
// ── VISIBILITY AND SHADOWING, neither of which is optional ──────────────────
// A fixture is resolved at the test's LOCATION, not globally, and exactly ONE wins.
// The runner looks in the test's own module first; failing that, in a conftest in its
// directory and then each ancestor directory, NEAREST first. It does not run the
// others. So emitting every same-named candidate is not a conservative union over
// things that might happen, it is a claim that a function runs which demonstrably does
// not -- the same fabrication measured at 114 of 601 edges on a public subject before
// this rule existed (see knobs.dl), and the shape resolution/value-flow.dl refuses when
// it declines "every class with a method called draw".
//
// py_fixture_conftest(Fixture, Dir) -- the directory a conftest governs.
// d KEEPS ITS TRAILING SEPARATOR, and that is load-bearing rather than untidy: for
// "tests/conftest.py" it is "tests/", so "tests_extra/foo.py" does not match, because it
// starts with "tests_". Strip the separator to tidy the string and every sibling
// directory sharing a prefix becomes governed by the wrong conftest.
//
// The comparison is >=, NOT >. A repo-root conftest.py has path == basename, so a
// strict > excludes it and the commonest place a project keeps shared fixtures
// contributes nothing at all. At the root d is "" and the prefix test admits every
// requester, which is exactly right.
.decl py_fixture_conftest(fix:symbol, dir:symbol)
py_fixture_conftest(fix, d) :- py_fixture_decl(fix, _),
    method_file("client", p, fix), py_fixture_scope_file(c),
    strlen(p) >= strlen(c), substr(p, strlen(p) - strlen(c), strlen(c)) = c,
    d = substr(p, 0, strlen(p) - strlen(c)).

// A requested parameter: no default, not a receiver, on a test or another fixture.
.decl py_fixture_request(req:symbol, name:symbol)
py_fixture_request(req, name) :- py_fixture_requester(req),
    param_decl("client", name, _, _, req, ph), name != "",
    param_default("client", "false", _, ph),
    param_kind("client", _, _, "false", ph).

// (a) the test's own module wins outright.
.decl py_fixture_same_module(req:symbol, name:symbol, fix:symbol)
py_fixture_same_module(req, name, fix) :- py_fixture_request(req, name),
    py_fixture_decl(fix, name), fix != req,
    method_file("client", p, req), method_file("client", p, fix).
.decl py_fixture_shadowed(req:symbol, name:symbol)
py_fixture_shadowed(req, name) :- py_fixture_same_module(req, name, _).

// (b) otherwise a conftest at or above the requester, NEAREST first. Depth is the
//     length of the governed directory, so the longest prefix is the nearest ancestor.
.decl py_fixture_conftest_cand(req:symbol, name:symbol, fix:symbol, depth:number)
py_fixture_conftest_cand(req, name, fix, dl) :- py_fixture_request(req, name),
    py_fixture_decl(fix, name), fix != req,
    py_fixture_conftest(fix, d), method_file("client", p, req),
    strlen(p) > strlen(d), substr(p, 0, strlen(d)) = d, dl = strlen(d).
.decl py_fixture_nearest(req:symbol, name:symbol, depth:number)
py_fixture_nearest(req, name, m) :- py_fixture_conftest_cand(req, name, _, _),
    m = max dl : { py_fixture_conftest_cand(req, name, _, dl) }.

.decl py_fixture_injection(from:symbol, to:symbol, name:symbol)
py_fixture_injection(req, fix, name) :- py_fixture_same_module(req, name, fix).
py_fixture_injection(req, fix, name) :- py_fixture_conftest_cand(req, name, fix, dl),
    !py_fixture_shadowed(req, name), py_fixture_nearest(req, name, dl).

.decl py_fixture_requested(m:symbol)
py_fixture_requested(m) :- py_fixture_injection(_, m, _).

framework_edge(from, to, "fixture_injection", name, "by_name") :-
    py_fixture_injection(from, to, name).

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 3. URL DISPATCH  (route table -> view)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// A routing table holds the view as a VALUE. The entry is a reference, not a call, so
// the view has no caller; the framework calls it on a request.
//
// THE TABLE IS REQUIRED. `path(...)` is an ordinary name, and a call to it outside a
// route table means nothing. The table is recognised by the module-level binding the
// framework reads, which is the one thing the framework itself specifies.
// BOTH keys are carried: call_arg is keyed by the CALL EXPRESSION (the site key in
// expression-resolution IS the expression hash, call-site.dl:55) while call_context is
// keyed by the site's own unique hash. They are different values and call_decl is the
// one relation that holds the pair.
.decl py_route_entry_call(site:symbol, expr:symbol)
py_route_entry_call(site, e) :-
    call_decl("client", _, fn, _, e, site), py_route_entry_fn(fn),
    expr_in_route_table(e).

// The VALUE side of `urlpatterns = [...]`. The table name is a STORE target and the
// entries are its sibling, not its descendant, so the two halves are joined through
// assign_pair rather than by walking up from the name.
.decl py_route_table_value(val:symbol)
py_route_table_value(val) :- assign_pair("client", tgt, val),
    expr_binding("client", b, "STORE", tgt),
    binding_decl("client", n, _, _, _, b), py_route_table(n).

// The entry expression IS that value, or sits transitively under it.
//
// SEEDED FROM THE TABLE, NOT FROM EVERY EXPRESSION. The obvious spelling is a general
// ancestor closure over expr_parent, but that materialises (expression x ancestor) for
// the WHOLE tree to answer a question about a handful of list literals. This descends
// from the table values only, so the closure is bounded by what is inside a routing
// table. (expr_parent's FIRST column is the provenance, not the parent; reading it as
// the parent makes every expression a child of "client".)
.decl expr_in_route_table(e:symbol)
expr_in_route_table(e) :- py_route_table_value(e).
expr_in_route_table(e) :- py_route_table_value(val), expr_parent("client", val, _, _, e).
expr_in_route_table(e) :- expr_in_route_table(p), expr_parent("client", p, _, _, e).

// The view is whichever argument of the entry DENOTES a def. A route entry takes the
// pattern as a string and the view as a reference, so the string arguments simply do
// not denote a method and drop out without a position rule.
.decl py_url_dispatch(from:symbol, to:symbol)
py_url_dispatch(from, to) :-
    py_route_entry_call(site, e), call_arg(e, _, a),
    expr_denotes_method("client", a, to), to != "",
    call_context("client", _, from, _, _, site), from != "".

entry_point(m, "url") :- py_url_dispatch(_, m).
framework_edge(from, to, "url_dispatch", "route_table", "registered") :-
    py_url_dispatch(from, to).

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 4. SIGNAL DISPATCH  (publisher -> receiver)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Both ends name the SIGNAL OBJECT and neither names the other. The join is therefore
// the binding the signal is held in -- the one thing both ends spell.
//
// A receiver is attached two ways, and both have to be modelled or half the receivers
// in a real project are invisible:
//   @receiver(sig)      the decorator, whose first argument names the signal
//   sig.connect(fn)     the imperative call, whose receiver names it
.decl py_signal_receiver(m:symbol, sigb:symbol)
// BOTH ENDS ARE NORMALISED THROUGH binding_lookup. The publisher and the receiver
// each write the signal's NAME, but a name reference carries its own binding hash per
// occurrence; only the DECLARING binding is shared. Joining the raw hashes silently
// produced no edge at all while both halves looked correct on their own.
py_signal_receiver(m, sigb) :-
    decorator_target("client", _, m, h), m != "",
    py_deco_named(h, v), py_signal_receiver_deco(v),
    decorator_expr("client", de, h), call_arg(de, "0", a),
    expr_binding("client", b, ctx, a), ctx != "STORE",
    binding_lookup("client", b, sigb).
py_signal_receiver(m, sigb) :-
    call_decl("client", _, meth, _, ce, site), py_signal_connect_method(meth),
    call_receiver("client", "NAME", _, rexpr, site),
    expr_binding("client", b, ctx, rexpr), ctx != "STORE",
    binding_lookup("client", b, sigb),
    call_arg(ce, "0", a), expr_denotes_method("client", a, m), m != "".

// A receiver runs when the signal fires, whether or not this tree contains the send.
entry_point(m, "signal_receiver") :- py_signal_receiver(m, _).

// The publisher: `<sig>.send(sender=...)` on the same binding.
.decl py_signal_send(site:symbol, from:symbol, sigb:symbol)
py_signal_send(site, from, sigb) :-
    call_decl("client", _, meth, _, _, site), py_signal_send_method(meth),
    call_receiver("client", "NAME", _, rexpr, site),
    expr_binding("client", b, ctx, rexpr), ctx != "STORE",
    binding_lookup("client", b, sigb),
    call_context("client", _, from, _, _, site), from != "".

// The two ends meet ONLY through the same binding. An ordinary `mailer.send(...)` has
// no receiver attached to that binding, so it joins nothing -- which is the whole
// reason the signal object, and not the method name, is the key.
framework_edge(from, to, "signal_dispatch", "signal", "registered") :-
    py_signal_send(_, from, sigb), py_signal_receiver(to, sigb).

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 5. DEPENDENCY-INJECTION PROVIDERS  (handler -> provider)
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// `def handler(svc = Depends(get_service))`. The marker receives the provider as a
// VALUE and the framework calls it. A provider may declare its own, so the chain has
// to carry.
//
// THE ARGUMENT MUST DENOTE A DEF. `Depends(get_service())` calls the provider eagerly:
// that is an ordinary call site, already a resolved edge, and the argument expression
// denotes the RESULT rather than the function -- so it drops out here with no rule of
// its own. `Depends()` names nothing and matches nothing.
.decl py_di_injection(from:symbol, to:symbol, marker:symbol)
py_di_injection(from, to, marker) :-
    param_decl("client", _, _, _, from, ph), from != "",
    param_default_expr("client", de, ph),
    call_decl("client", _, marker, _, de, _), py_di_marker(marker),
    call_arg(de, "0", a),
    expr_denotes_method("client", a, to), to != "", to != from.

entry_point(m, "di_provider") :- py_di_injection(_, m, _).
framework_edge(from, to, "di_provider", marker, "registered") :-
    py_di_injection(from, to, marker).

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 6. ORM AND MODEL HOOKS
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// A lifecycle or validation hook is registered by decoration and called by the library.
// There is no second end in the client at all -- nothing here fires the hook -- so this
// mechanism produces an ENTRY POINT and no framework_edge. Claiming an edge would mean
// naming a caller that does not exist.
//
// The registration must NAME ITS TARGET: a hook always says what it hooks (a mapper and
// an event, or the fields it validates). A project's own decorator spelled the same way
// takes no such argument, and a bare call to a function of that name is not a
// registration at all.
.decl py_orm_hook(m:symbol, tail:symbol)
py_orm_hook(m, v) :- decorator_target("client", _, m, h), m != "",
    py_deco_named(h, v), py_orm_hook_deco(v),
    decorator_text("client", _, _, argc, h), argc != "0", argc != "".

entry_point(m, "orm_hook") :- py_orm_hook(m, _).

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 7. DIAGNOSTICS — the half that says what did NOT join
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// A dispatch-shaped site whose receiver is not a registered task, and a receiver
// attached to a signal nothing sends. Both are reported rather than silently dropped,
// so "the engine saw it and declined" is distinguishable from "the engine never looked".
framework_unjoined(site, "task_dispatch", meth) :-
    call_decl("client", _, meth, _, _, site), py_task_dispatch_method(meth),
    !py_task_dispatch(site, _, _, _).
framework_unjoined(site, "signal_dispatch", "no_receiver") :-
    py_signal_send(site, _, sigb), !py_signal_receiver(_, sigb).
